.TH "OSMIUM" "1" "1.19.1" "" ""
.SH NAME
osmium \- multipurpose tool for working with OpenStreetMap data
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]osmium\f[R] \f[I]COMMAND\f[R] [\f[I]ARG\f[R]\&...]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]osmium\f[R] \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
Multipurpose tool for working with OpenStreetMap data.
.PP
Run \f[B]osmium help\f[R] \f[I]COMMAND\f[R] to get more information
about a command.
This will only work on Linux and macOS systems and only if the
\f[CR]man\f[R] command is available and working correctly.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\-h, \-\-help
Show usage and list of commands.
.TP
\-\-version
Show program version.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
add\-locations\-to\-ways
add node locations to ways in OSM file
.TP
apply\-changes
apply OSM change file(s) to OSM data file
.TP
cat
concatenate OSM files and convert to different formats
.TP
changeset\-filter
filter changesets from OSM changeset files
.TP
check\-refs
check referential integrity of OSM file
.TP
derive\-changes
create OSM change file from two OSM files
.TP
diff
display differences between OSM files
.TP
export
export OSM data
.TP
extract
create geographical extracts from an OSM file
.TP
fileinfo
show information about an OSM file
.TP
getid
get objects from OSM file by ID
.TP
getparents
get parents of objects from OSM file
.TP
help
show help about commands
.TP
merge
merge several OSM files into one
.TP
merge\-changes
merge several OSM change files into one
.TP
removeid
remove OSM objects with specified IDs
.TP
renumber
renumber object IDs
.TP
show
show OSM file
.TP
sort
sort OSM files
.TP
tags\-filter
filter OSM data based on tags
.TP
time\-filter
filter OSM data by time from a history file
.SH COMMON OPTIONS
Most commands support the following options:
.TP
\-h, \-\-help
Show short usage information.
.TP
\-v, \-\-verbose
Set verbose mode.
The program will output information about what it is doing to STDERR.
.SH MEMORY USAGE
Osmium commands try to do their work as memory efficient as possible.
But some osmium commands still need to load quite a bit of data into
main memory.
In some cases this means that only smaller datasets can be handled.
Look into the man pages for the individual commands to learn more about
their memory use.
.PP
On most commands, if you use the \f[B]\-\-verbose/\-v\f[R] option,
osmium will print out the peak memory usage at the end.
This is the actual amount of memory used including the program code
itself, any needed libraries, and the data.
(Printing of memory usage is currently only available on Linux systems.)
.PP
If an osmium command exits with an \(lqOut of memory\(rq error, try
running it with \f[B]\-\-verbose/\-v\f[R] on smaller datasets to get an
idea how much memory it needs.
.PP
On Linux a program that uses a lot of memory can be killed by the kernel
without the program being notified.
If you see osmium dieing without any apparent reason, this might be the
case.
Search on the Internet for \(lqOOM killer\(rq to find out more about
this.
